(iii) dreamed speed / true speed (the law says 1.000)
       tau        0       24       60      120      199   horizon
      0.00    1.035    0.957    1.009    1.195      nan      14.6
      0.50    2.381    1.491    0.864    0.590      nan       2.1
      1.00    7.641    5.182    2.365    1.220      nan       0.4
     floor    0.955    0.925    0.964    0.981      nan      30.9
    'horizon' = mean first step at which the dreamed speed is off the law by > 25% (max 190)
    At tau > 0 the RATIO is inflated by per-step sampling jitter that the position
    probe cannot separate from motion. 
    (the across-episode speed correlation is undefined here: in v1 every
    episode has the SAME true speed, so there is no variance to correlate with.)

(vi) v4: the gravity SIGN along the dream -- the conserved quantity here.
    A frozen linear probe on h, 0.594 accurate on real teacher-forced passes, read along a 190-step dream.
       tau  consistency  (all steps)   steps the model calls a contact
      0.00        0.511        0.513                             0.017
      0.50        0.528        0.527                             0.001
      1.00        0.506        0.505                             0.001
    'consistency' = fraction of dreamed steps whose decoded sign still matches the
    one in force when the dream began, over steps at which the model's own hit head
    does not think a contact is happening.

(iv) fraction of dreamed frames with a well-formed ball (area within 50-150% of the VAE reference)
    tau=0.00  mean over the dream 0.994   at the end 0.967
    tau=0.50  mean over the dream 0.839   at the end 0.900
    tau=1.00  mean over the dream 0.882   at the end 0.933

In plain English:
  At step 150 the dreamed ball moves at 2.14 x the speed the law gives it.
  The dream stays inside +-25% of the law for 0 steps on average (the
  measuring probe's own floor is 31).  88% of the dreamed frames still
  contain a ball of roughly the right size.
  At tau=0.0 (deterministic) the same numbers are speed ratio 1.33, speed horizon 15.
  The gap between the two rows is the cost of sampling, which is the whole
  finding: the model KNOWS the quantity is constant, and loses it anyway
  as soon as it has to feed on its own draws.

